\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{An explicit conditional Gaussian kernel}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{In the orthogonal positive setting of
[Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D), let #{X} be retained and #{Y}
eliminated. The components of #{Q} are #{A,B,B^\dagger,C}.
Put #{T=C-B^\dagger A^{-1}B} and
#{c_A=(2\pi)^{\dim X/2}/\sqrt{\det A}}. The maps #{A,T} are
positive definite by [exchanging the eliminated summand](fdip-001Y).
For every #{y\in Y}, define a probability measure on #{X} by
##{\begin{aligned}
&k_y(x)=c_A^{-1}\exp\bigl(-\tfrac12\\
&\qquad\cdot h_X(x+A^{-1}By,A(x+A^{-1}By))\bigr),\\
&\kappa(y,D)=\int_D k_y(x)\,d\lambda_X(x)
\end{aligned}}
for Borel #{D\subseteq X}. These measures form a measurable
\newvocab{conditional probability kernel} for the retained field
given the eliminated field. Its mean is #{-A^{-1}By} and covariance
operator is #{A^{-1}}.}

\proof{
\p{Exchanged square completion gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&h_E(J(x,y),QJ(x,y))\\
&\quad=h_X(x+A^{-1}By,A(x+A^{-1}By))\\
&\qquad+h_Y(y,Ty).
\end{aligned}}
The joint density relative to #{\lambda_X\otimes\lambda_Y} is
#{p_Q(x,y)=Z_Q^{-1}w_Q(J(x,y))}. Its #{Y}-marginal density is
##{\begin{aligned}
r(y)&=\int_Xp_Q(x,y)\,d\lambda_X(x)\\
 &=\frac{c_A}{Z_Q}e^{-h_Y(y,Ty)/2}>0.
\end{aligned}}
It is continuous, finite and integrates to one by Tonelli.}
\p{Division gives #{k_y(x)=p_Q(x,y)/r(y)}. Translation invariance and
[positive normalization](fdip-002T) prove
#{\int_Xk_y\,d\lambda_X=1} for every #{y}, including zero-dimensional
spaces. Thus #{\kappa(y,\cdot)} is a probability measure.}
\p{For fixed Borel #{D}, the function
#{(y,x)\mapsto\mathbf1_D(x)k_y(x)} is nonnegative Borel.
Measurability of parameter integrals for sigma-finite volume shows
that #{y\mapsto\kappa(y,D)} is Borel. This is the measurable-parameter
part of
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure},
applied to the uncompleted Borel product.}
\p{For Borel #{D\subseteq X} and #{H\subseteq Y}, Tonelli and
#{k_yr=p_Q} give
##{\mu_Q(J(D\times H))
 =\int_H\kappa(y,D)r(y)\,d\lambda_Y(y).}
The set on the left is Borel because #{J} is a homeomorphism.
This rectangle identity is the conditional-distribution identity
with respect to the actual #{Y}-marginal. It divides densities,
not the probability of the event #{Y=y}; in positive dimension the
latter singleton has probability zero. Our version is defined for
every #{y}, whereas conditional distributions in general are
identified only almost everywhere.}
\p{Under #{\kappa(y,\cdot)}, the translated variable
#{u=x+A^{-1}By} has centered precision #{A}. Its mean is zero and
its covariance is #{A^{-1}} by \ref{fdip-000B}. Translating back
proves the stated mean and covariance.}
}

\p{The [retained marginal](fdip-000E) instead has mean zero and
covariance #{S^{-1}} for #{S=A-BC^{-1}B^\dagger}. At #{y=0}, the
conditional precision is #{A}, not #{S}. If #{B=0}, then #{S=A}
and the conditional mean is zero for every #{y}, so the laws coincide.
The [four-cycle](fdip-000F) exhibits their different off-diagonal
covariances when the coupling is nonzero.}
\p{To compare covariance parameters, put #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}} and
write its components as #{\Sigma_{XX},\Sigma_{XY},\Sigma_{YY}}.
The [opposite-pivot inverse formulas](fdip-001V) give
##{\begin{aligned}
\Sigma_{YY}&=T^{-1},\\
\Sigma_{XY}&=-A^{-1}BT^{-1},\\
\Sigma_{XX}&=A^{-1}+A^{-1}BT^{-1}B^\dagger A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Consequently
##{\begin{aligned}
\Sigma_{XY}\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}&=-A^{-1}B,\\
\Sigma_{XX}-\Sigma_{XY}\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}\Sigma_{YX}&=A^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The covariance Schur complement for conditioning differs from the
precision Schur complement for marginalization.}
\p{\citet{Appendix A.2, equation (A.6), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
gives these conditional Gaussian parameters. The density construction
above additionally checks normalization, parameter measurability and
the conditional integral identity.}
\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.condDistrib_multivariateGaussian}
identifies a conditional distribution with its Gaussian kernel almost
everywhere for the conditioning marginal, assuming a Gaussian joint
law, positive-semidefinite covariance and a positive-definite
conditioning covariance block. Here #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}>0} and
#{\Sigma_{YY}=T^{-1}>0}; the displayed identities supply the parameter
conversion. The totalized Gaussian constructor or a Markov-kernel
instance alone would not identify the conditional distribution.}
